  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather with warmer than average temperatures\nthrough Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure remains centered just east of the area. The high\ncenter is close enough east of us to where all of the area will\nremain with strong enough subsidence to keep us dry, with the \nexception of isolated showers and storms right along the Quebec \nborder of the western North Woods the next few afternoon. Winds \nwill remain fairly light with sea breezes up to about 10 mph. \nTemperatures will be warm, but not hot, with highs in the upper \n70s to mid 80s. Dewpoints will remain relatively low, especially\nin the afternoon hours with upper 40s to mid 50s, so it won't \nfeel muggy. Patchy valley fog will again be likely tonight and \ntomorrow night, but should dissipate not long after the sun \ncomes up.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A system is likely to move in mid-week, \nbringing another round of wetting rainfall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system slides along the east coast this week. \nGenerally, low pressure east of the Carolinas moves northeast in\nthe Atlantic, east of Nova Scotia. Meanwhile, a cold front \napproaches on Monday and lingers to our west on Tuesday. A \nshortwave diving southeast over the Ontario province will \ndevelop a low pressure system over the Great Lakes region late \nMonday into Tuesday. Wetting rainfall expected, with higher \ntotals along Downeast, Wednesday morning into Thursday. \nPotential for 0.5 to 1 inch of rainfall. Some thunderstorms are \nalso possible with this system Wednesday afternoon into \nThursday. There is some instability (CAPE, good low-level lapse \nrates, DCAPE) ahead of the front, so potential for isolated \nthunderstorms with some gusty winds.",
